<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ruleset name="Display Builder PHPMD Rules"
         xmlns="http://pmd.sf.net/ruleset/1.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sf.net/ruleset/1.0.0
                     http://pmd.sf.net/ruleset_xml_schema.xsd"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="
                     http://pmd.sf.net/ruleset_xml_schema.xsd">
    <description>
        Display Builder PHPMD Rules.

        Deliberately not a full ruleset. Much of PHPMD predates PHP 8 and
        assumes Java idioms, so what is enabled below is the subset that
        survives contact with Drupal. Every threshold is set explicitly, even
        where it matches the stock value, so this file documents its own
        intent rather than deferring to a default that can move.

        What is excluded, and why, is listed at the bottom. Read it before
        adding a rule back.
    </description>

    <!-- Dead code. Free signal, no false positive worth the name. -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/unusedcode.xml">
      <!-- Excluded to use it with a different configuration. -->
      <exclude name="UnusedLocalVariable" />
    </rule>
    <rule ref="rulesets/unusedcode.xml/UnusedLocalVariable">
      <properties>
        <property name="allow-unused-foreach-variables" value="true" />
      </properties>
    </rule>

    <!--
        Branching. 12 rather than the stock 10: nullable storage loads, render
        array defaults and type guards all cost branches Drupal cannot avoid,
        and guard clauses raise this number while lowering nesting, which is
        the shape we want people writing. Fires at greater-or-equal, so this
        allows 11. Do not raise it further; past about 15 it catches nothing.
        Treat a hit as a question - does this method do two jobs? - rather
        than as something to suppress.
    -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml/CyclomaticComplexity">
        <properties>
            <property name="reportLevel" value="12" />
        </properties>
    </rule>

    <!--
        Execution paths through a method. Catches the combinatorial case
        CyclomaticComplexity misses, and stays quiet on a flat chain of early
        returns.
    -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml/NPathComplexity">
        <properties>
            <property name="minimum" value="200" />
        </properties>
    </rule>

    <!--
        Size. Raised over stock: a method assembling a render array is long
        without being complex, and a plugin class carries its attribute, its
        create() and a whole interface in one file.
    -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml/ExcessiveMethodLength">
        <properties>
            <property name="minimum" value="120" />
        </properties>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml/ExcessiveClassLength">
        <properties>
            <property name="minimum" value="1200" />
        </properties>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml/ExcessiveClassComplexity">
        <properties>
            <property name="maximum" value="60" />
        </properties>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml/ExcessiveParameterList">
        <properties>
            <property name="minimum" value="12" />
        </properties>
    </rule>

    <!-- Things that are never right in a Drupal module. -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/design.xml/ExitExpression" />
    <rule ref="rulesets/design.xml/EvalExpression" />
    <rule ref="rulesets/design.xml/GotoStatement" />
    <!-- Swallowing a throwable is sometimes correct here, saying why is not
         optional. @see ViewPanelBase::createSlotSource(), which logs. -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/design.xml/EmptyCatchBlock" />

    <!-- Cheap correctness. -->
    <rule ref="rulesets/cleancode.xml/DuplicatedArrayKey" />
    <rule ref="rulesets/cleancode.xml/ErrorControlOperator" />

    <!--
        Deliberately excluded. Do not add one back without reading this.

        cleancode/StaticAccess - \Drupal::, Url::fromRoute(),
          Html::cleanCssIdentifier(). Drupal is built on static access.
        cleancode/BooleanArgumentFlag - core signatures are full of
          bool $reset = FALSE, and ours match core on purpose.
        cleancode/ElseExpression - bans else outright. Dogma, not signal.
        design/CouplingBetweenObjects - a plugin importing fifteen core
          classes is normal, not coupled.
        design/DepthOfInheritance - plugin hierarchies are several levels
          deep before our own code starts.
        codesize/TooManyPublicMethods - an interface is allowed to be wide.
          @see DisplayBuildableInterface.
        codesize/TooManyFields - one property per injected service.
        naming/* - LongVariable against $target_entity_type_id,
          ShortVariable against $e in every catch block.
        controversial/* - the CamelCase rules fire on every hook in a
          .module file.

        Two more, tried against this codebase and dropped on the numbers:
        cleancode/IfStatementAssignment fires 20 times. `if ($x = foo())` is
          used deliberately and consistently here, so the rule buys 20
          permanent baseline entries and changes nothing.
        design/DevelopmentCodeFragment fires 3 times, every one of them a
          print_r($x, TRUE) building a watchdog message. The rule cannot tell
          a logged dump from a forgotten one.

        Two judgment calls, left off rather than half-adopted:
        cleancode/MissingImport is good hygiene but fires on inline
        \Throwable, which we use. codesize/TooManyMethods is real signal for
        a god class but costs baseline entries on day one.

        PHPMD will never catch the duplication this codebase actually grows:
        the same small helper written twice in two plugin classes. phpcpd
        covers that, configured in .phpqa.yml.
    -->
</ruleset>
